Detect secrets that look real (AWS keys, tokens, etc.).

`evnx scan rules …` manages rule files and `evnx scan hook …` a git pre-commit hook. A folder literally named `rules` or `hook` is scanned as `evnx scan ./rules`.

Usage: evnx scan [OPTIONS] [PATH]...
       evnx scan <COMMAND>

Commands:
  rules  Your own scan rules: list, create and test them
  hook   A git `pre-commit` hook that runs `evnx scan --staged`
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PATH]...
          Files or folders to scan. Repeatable; defaults to the current folder.
          
          A folder is walked. A file named here is always read — whatever `--exclude`, `--extensions` or the size limit say — and always reported in full, even one git ignores. `-` reads stdin, in memory (`git diff | evnx scan -`): as a `.env` when it parses as one, otherwise as text. Empty stdin is an error, not a clean result.
          
          [default: .]

Options:
      --exclude <EXCLUDE>
          Skip paths in a folder walk. Repeatable; adds to `[scan] exclude`.
          
          A value with `*`, `?` or `[` is a glob, matched against the path from the folder and against the file name: `'fixtures/**'`, `'*.log'`. Any other value matches a whole path component, or several in a row: `--exclude test` skips `test/` and a file named `test`, not `latest/` or `contest.py`; `third_party/cache` skips that folder wherever it is.
          
          This narrows the scan. Below the folder scanned, `.git`, `node_modules`, `target`, `dist`, `build`, `.next`, `.nuxt`, `.turbo`, `__pycache__`, Python virtualenvs (a `venv` or `.venv` holding `pyvenv.cfg`) and dependency `vendor` folders (beside `go.mod`, `composer.json` or `Gemfile`, or holding `modules.txt`) are skipped, unless git tracks files inside them, and so is the `.env.example` family; `--include-dir` walks a skipped folder. A file named on the command line is read regardless.
          
          [default: .env.example]

      --max-file-size <SIZE>
          Skip walked files larger than this: `500K`, `5MB`, `2MiB`.
          
          Defaults to 1 MiB, or `[scan] max_file_size` from `.evnx.toml`. A skipped file is counted and, with `-v`, listed. A file named on the command line is read whatever its size.

      --extensions <EXT>
          Read only files with these extensions in folders (comma-separated: `env,yml,json`). By default every file is read, and binary or very large files are skipped with a note.
          
          This narrows the scan, and the run says so. A file named on the command line is read whatever this says. `[scan] extensions` in `.evnx.toml` sets the same thing for a project.

      --pattern <REGEX>
          Also report values matching this regular expression. Repeatable.
          
          For the formats evnx does not ship with — an internal token, a vendor key shape. `--pattern 'ACME-[A-Z0-9]{32}'` reports matches as `Custom pattern 1`, numbered in the order the flags are given.
          
          It takes an expression and nothing else. For a name that means something, and for a rule the whole team scans with, declare it under `[[scan.patterns]]` in `.evnx.toml` instead; both sets apply together.
          
          An expression that does not compile exits 2 — no verdict — rather than reporting a clean scan it never ran.

      --ignore-placeholders
          Skip values evnx recognises as filler.
          
          `changeme`, `your_api_key_here`, `xxx` and similar. Useful on a first run over a template-heavy repository; prefer `--severity high` if the goal is simply less noise.

      --no-ignore-placeholders
          Report filler-looking values even when `[scan] ignore_placeholders` is `true`. The last of the two flags wins

      --severity <LEVEL>
          Lowest severity worth reporting: critical, high, medium or low.
          
          Severity is how bad a finding is if it is real: `critical` for cloud, payment and AI keys and private keys, `high` for account tokens, database passwords and variables named like a secret, `low` for test keys and keys meant to ship in a web page. Filters what is reported, counted and exited on. `--severity high` is the usual blocking CI gate. Until 0.13.0 this filtered on confidence; that is now `--confidence`. Defaults to `low` (report everything), or `[scan] severity` from `.evnx.toml`.
          
          [possible values: critical, high, medium, low]

      --rules <FILE>
          A rule file for this run, on top of `.evnx/rules/` and your personal rules. Repeatable. Same format as those: see `evnx scan rules new`

      --baseline <FILE>
          Findings accepted earlier: matching ones are reported as `known` and do not fail the run.
          
          Defaults to `.evnx/baseline.json` when it exists. Write it with `--update-baseline`. It holds a fingerprint, a rule and a file per finding, never a value, so it is safe to commit.

      --update-baseline
          Write this run's findings to the baseline (`--baseline`, or `.evnx/baseline.json`) and exit 0. Run it once to accept what a project already has; new findings fail from then on

      --reason <CODE>
          Why the findings `--update-baseline` adds are accepted: `used-in-tests`, `false-positive` or `fix-later`.
          
          Stored in the baseline for the entries added by this run; entries already there keep their reason. The summary counts allowed and known findings by it. Inline, write `evnx:allow <rule> reason=used-in-tests`.

      --confidence <LEVEL>
          Lowest confidence worth reporting: high, medium or low.
          
          Confidence is how sure evnx is that a match is a real credential of that kind. Combine with `--severity`: both must pass. Defaults to `low`, or `[scan] confidence` from `.evnx.toml`.
          
          [possible values: high, medium, low]

      --exit-zero
          Always exit 0, even when secrets are found.
          
          Suppresses exit 1 only. A scan that could not run still exits 2 — the flag means "do not fail my build over findings", not "never tell me the scan was impossible" (an invalid `--exclude`, a path that does not exist, nothing to scan). Files that could not be read are listed and counted but are findings-class (exit 1), so this flag lets them pass.

      --include-ignored
          Report findings in files git ignores and does not track.
          
          In a git repository a folder walk counts what git would commit: a filled-in `.env` that `.gitignore` covers is where secrets belong, so its findings are summarised in one line and do not fail the run. This lists them as findings instead. A file named on the command line, and any folder outside a repository, is always reported in full.

      --staged
          Scan what is staged for the next commit, read from the git index.
          
          A file staged and then edited on disk is scanned as staged, because that is what the commit records. Paths limit it to staged files under them. Nothing staged exits 0. `evnx scan hook install` runs this before every commit.

      --history [<RANGE>]
          Scan the files git history added or changed, not the files on disk.
          
          Takes any revision range: `--history origin/main..HEAD`, `--history v0.15.0..`. Bare `--history` is everything reachable from `HEAD`, and `--history all` every ref. Each distinct file version is read once, whole (so a name rule sees its key), and reported against the oldest commit that has it: `path@abc1234:LINE`. A secret that was added and later removed is still found: rotate it, then rewrite history. Paths limit it to files under them. Exits 2 outside a repository.

      --full
          With a bare `--history`: scan all of history again.
          
          A bare `--history` builds on the last clean scan of this clone (kept in `.git/evnx/history.json`, never committed) and reads only the commits made since, unless the rules or the scan settings changed. `--full` reads everything.

      --no-config
          Ignore the repository's `.evnx.toml` and use the built-in defaults.
          
          For a CI gate that must not depend on settings the scanned change can edit: no disabled rules, no severity floor, no excludes, no default `.evnx/baseline.json`. A `--baseline` you name is still read.
          
          Still in force: rule files in `.evnx/rules/`, your personal rules and `--rules` files, which can only add rules (a rule's own `allow` lists apply to that rule alone; none can disable or replace one of evnx's), and `evnx:allow` comments inside the scanned files, which silence the line they sit on (`-v` lists them).

      --include-dir <NAME>
          Walk a folder the scan skips by default, by name. Repeatable.
          
          `--include-dir vendor` reads a `vendor/` folder, `--include-dir .venv` a virtualenv. Needed only for the folders the run lists as "skipped dependency folders", or `.next` and the like.

      --follow-symlinks
          Read a symlinked file in a folder walk when its target is inside the scanned folder.
          
          By default a symlink is skipped and counted. A link to a file outside the folder, or to a folder, is skipped either way.

      --policy <FILE>
          A policy file the repository's `.evnx.toml` cannot lower.
          
          Read after `.evnx.toml`, and also with `--no-config`. TOML with three optional keys: `required_rules` (detectors that may not be disabled), `max_severity_floor` (the highest `--severity` or `[scan] severity` allowed) and `allow_disable` (`false`, or the only detectors a repository may disable). A setting that conflicts exits 2 before any file is read, naming the key and this file. A missing or invalid file exits 2. Also read from `EVNX_POLICY`; the flag wins. A hook installed by `evnx scan hook install` honours `EVNX_POLICY` too.
          
          [env: EVNX_POLICY=]

      --strict
          Treat an unknown key in `.evnx.toml` as an error (exit 2).
          
          Without it an unknown key is a warning, with a "did you mean" when it is close to a known one, and the key is ignored. A misspelt `severty` then lowers nothing and raises nothing; for a CI gate that is better stopped.

      --exit-only
          Print nothing; the exit code is the answer: 0 clean, 1 findings, 2 could not run.
          
          Not even an error message or the config line: for a script that only branches on the code. The global `-q` still prints findings. Cannot be combined with `--format` or `--json`. A usage error from the command line itself is still printed.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: `pretty`, `json`, `sarif` or `github`.
          
          `sarif` uploads to GitHub's Security tab; `github` emits `::error` annotations inline on a pull request; `json` carries a `summary` object for `jq`.
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json, sarif, github]

      --json
          Same as --format json: one JSON document with a `summary` object for `jq`.

What a folder walk reads:
  every file, except .git, node_modules, target, dist and build, the
  .env.example family, editor swap files, binary files (except PKCS#12
  keystores, found by their content), and files over
  1 MiB (--max-file-size). A file named on the command line is always read.
  In a git repository, findings in files git ignores and does not track
  are summarised, not counted (--include-ignored counts them).

Encoded values:
  a value of 16 to 4096 characters that is base64, hex or percent-encoded
  text is decoded once and checked against the rules below only (not names
  or entropy). The finding shows "encoded" in JSON.

Context:
  a value on a line with an Authorization or Bearer header, an HTTP client
  call or a connection string is shown one level higher; a file under a
  test, tests, mock, spec or fixtures folder one level lower (name, entropy
  and medium findings only). Nothing is dropped, and the exit code and
  --confidence use the level before the hint.

Accepting a finding:
  evnx:allow <rule> reason=used-in-tests|false-positive|fix-later, then
  free text, on the line or the one above; --update-baseline --reason CODE
  does the same for the baseline. The summary counts by reason.

Rules, by the names [scan] disable takes (or by id: evnx scan -v --format json):
  AWS Access Key, AWS Temporary Access Key (STS), GitHub Fine-grained PAT,
  Slack Token, Google API Key, JSON Web Token, Stripe Secret Key (LIVE),
  Clerk Secret Key (LIVE), UploadThing Secret Key, Stripe Secret Key (test),
  GitHub Token, OpenAI API Key, Anthropic API Key,
  OpenSSH Private Key, Private Key, Credentials in a connection string,
  Stripe Restricted Key (LIVE), Azure Storage Account Key, Azure SAS Token,
  Twilio API Key, SendGrid API Key, Mailgun API Key, DigitalOcean Token,
  npm Access Token, PyPI API Token, Docker Hub Access Token,
  Shopify Access Token, HashiCorp Vault Token, GitLab Personal Access Token,
  Hugging Face Token, Heroku API Key, Sentry DSN, Sentry Auth Token,
  Linear API Key, Discord Bot Token, Telegram Bot Token, Slack Webhook URL,
  Google OAuth Client Secret, GitLab Runner Authentication Token, GitLab
  Deploy Token, GitLab Pipeline Trigger Token, Groq API Key, xAI API Key,
  Replicate API Token, Perplexity API Key, Stripe Webhook Signing Secret,
  Databricks Personal Access Token, Atlassian API Token, Bitbucket App
  Password, HCP Terraform API Token, Pulumi Access Token, Doppler Token,
  Supabase Personal Access Token, Supabase Secret Key, age Secret Key, New
  Relic User API Key, Grafana Service Account Token, Grafana Cloud Access
  Policy Token, Postman API Key, Notion Integration Token, GitLab CI/CD
  Job Token, Fly.io Access Token, Azure DevOps Personal Access Token,
  GitLab Feed Token, GitLab Incoming Email Token, GitLab Agent for
  Kubernetes Token, GitLab Webhook Token, GitLab SCIM Token, GitLab Feature
  Flags Client Token, GitLab OAuth Application Secret;
  and the heuristics: Sensitive config key, High-entropy string,
  AWS Secret Access Key.

A short value under a secret name (PASSWORD=admin, 4 to 7 characters) is
reported as Sensitive config key, medium severity and low confidence, only
when --confidence low (or [scan] confidence = "low") is given.

A policy the repository cannot lower:
  evnx scan --policy ci/scan-policy.toml        (or EVNX_POLICY)
  required_rules = ["AWS Access Key"]    detectors .evnx.toml may not disable
  max_severity_floor = "medium"          highest --severity or [scan] severity
  allow_disable = false | ["Slack Token"]   what .evnx.toml may disable
  A conflict exits 2 before any file is read, naming the key and the policy.
  --strict makes an unknown key in .evnx.toml exit 2; --exit-only prints
  nothing and answers with the exit code: 0 clean, 1 findings, 2 could not run.

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    1  Ran, and found something: findings, problems, a difference, not in sync
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/scan
